        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Borobudur</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 780 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Begun under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra dynasty</a> about 780 and completed under the Sanjaya about 833, in Jacques Dumarçay's dating; no record of its building survives, and its architect, Gunadharma, is named only in Javanese folk tales</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A single great stupa of grey andesite on a natural hill, laid out as a mandala: nine stacked platforms, six square and three circular, rising to a central dome 35 m above the ground, with 2,672 relief panels and originally 504 Buddha statues</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Magelang Regency, near Muntilan, northwest of Yogyakarta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>; sculptures and Buddha heads taken from it are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> National Museum, the Tropenmuseum, the Musée Guimet and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the largest Buddhist temple in the world; 1,460 narrative relief panels; 72 Buddhas seated in perforated stupas; the hidden foot with the Karmavibhanga reliefs; the relief of the Borobudur Ship; the Vesak walk from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendut">Mendut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawon">Pawon</a></span></div>
