    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Borobudur was built as one great stupa in the form of a mandala, its stairways and corridors leading pilgrims upward past 1,460 narrative reliefs to 72 Buddhas in perforated stupas.</li><li>Abandoned at a date nobody knows and buried under volcanic ash and jungle, but never forgotten locally, it was cleared in 1814 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Stamford Raffles</a>, who brought it to the world's attention.</li><li>The restoration of 1975–1982 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> took down and reset over one million stones, and the temple is again a place of pilgrimage at Vesak.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1896 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> of Siam was allowed to take home eight cartloads of sculptures from Borobudur; several of them, among them lions and a guardian statue, are now in the National Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

